Here is how HTMX 4 handles request cancellation and hx-sync during rapid typing, the key Firefox vs. Chrome DOM event quirks on <input> and <select> controls, and a tiny Go server to run the standalone repro locally.

1. HTMX 4 Request Cancellation & hx-sync During Rapid Typing
By default, overlapping requests do NOT cancel each other: Without hx-sync, every timer tick from hx-trigger="input changed delay:200ms" spawns an independent fetch() request. If an older request finishes after a newer one, the older response overwrites the newer DOM.

How hx-sync="this:replace" prevents races: In HTMX 4, hx-sync="SELECTOR:STRATEGY" coordinates competing requests on a shared queue. With this:replace, when a new keystroke debounce fires while the previous request is still in flight, HTMX aborts the running fetch() via AbortController and starts the latest request.

Shared queues across two controls (hx-sync="#control-a:replace"): If Control A and Control B both update #results, pointing both at the same sync target (hx-sync="#shared-form:replace" or hx-sync="#control-a:replace") ensures whichever control triggered last cancels the other's in-flight request.

Manual cancellation (htmx:abort): Dispatching htmx:abort on an element programmatically cancels its active request (htmx.trigger('#control-a', 'htmx:abort')).   
htmx

2. Firefox vs. Chrome DOM Event Quirks on <input>, <select>, and type="search"
When two controls behave differently in Firefox versus Chromium during keyboard interaction, one of these four browser differences is usually responsible:

<input type="search"> clear (Escape / clear icon): Chromium fires input and search reliably when clearing via the built-in × button or Escape, whereas Firefox does not show Chromium's built-in × clear button and handles Escape differently depending on whether an autocomplete popup is open.

<select> keyboard navigation (ArrowUp / ArrowDown):

Firefox fires change (and input) on every arrow-key step while browsing options in an unexpanded <select>, whereas Chromium defers change until Enter or blur in several modes.

If you listen to change on a <select> without delay:... or hx-sync, arrowing through 5 options in Firefox fires 5 rapid requests.

IME / composition keyup ordering: Firefox fires the trailing keyup for Enter or space even when that keystroke just committed an IME composition (compositionend), whereas Chromium suppresses that incidental keyup. Using input instead of keyup avoids false triggers.

Focus transfer between keydown and keyup: If a handler on Control A moves .focus() to a <select> during keydown, Firefox can still apply the subsequent key/character handling to the newly focused <select>, changing its selected value unexpectedly.

3. Tiny Go Server for the Standalone Firefox Repro
Drop this single-file Go server next to your repro HTML (or serve the HTML inline) with simulated latency (?delay=150ms) so you can watch hx-sync cancellations and event timestamps in Firefox and Chrome DevTools:

Go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"html"
	"log"
	"net/http"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()

	// Serve the standalone repro HTML page.
	mux.HandleFunc("/", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		http.ServeFile(w, r, "repro.html")
	})

	// Mock HTMX endpoint with configurable artificial delay to expose race conditions.
	mux.HandleFunc("/mock-search", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		if d, err := time.ParseDuration(r.URL.Query().Get("delay")); err == nil && d > 0 {
			select {
			case <-time.After(d):
			case <-r.Context().Done():
				// Client (htmx hx-sync="...:replace") aborted this request.
				log.Printf("aborted request: q=%q type=%q source=%q",
					r.URL.Query().Get("q"),
					r.URL.Query().Get("type"),
					r.Header.Get("HX-Source"),
				)
				return
			}
		}

		q := html.EscapeString(r.URL.Query().Get("q"))
		typ := html.EscapeString(r.URL.Query().Get("type"))
		src := html.EscapeString(r.Header.Get("HX-Source"))

		w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "text/html; charset=utf-8")
		fmt.Fprintf(w, `<p>Response at %s — q=%q, type=%q, HX-Source=%q</p>`,
			time.Now().Format("15:04:05.000"), q, typ, src)
	})

	log.Println("Listening on http://127.0.0.1:8099")
	log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe("127.0.0.1:8099", mux))
}
