what is OOB

OOB stands for Out-of-Band (hx-swap-oob in HTMX).

Normally, an HTMX request updates one primary target on the page—either the element that triggered the request or the element specified in hx-target="#tuple-list".

An Out-of-Band swap lets a single server response update additional, unrelated parts of the page at the same time, without nesting everything inside one giant container.

Concrete Example from go-topaz-ui
Suppose the user types into #object-search (hx-target="#tuple-list"):

Primary swap: The server returns the new list items, which HTMX puts inside #tuple-list.

OOB swaps: In the same HTTP response, the server includes two top-level elements marked with hx-swap-oob:

HTML
<!-- 1. Main content: goes into hx-target="#tuple-list" -->
<ul>
  <li>document:doc-1 — viewer — user:alice</li>
</ul>

<!-- 2. OOB swap: HTMX finds id="tuple-count" elsewhere on the page and replaces its innerHTML -->
<span id="tuple-count" hx-swap-oob="innerHTML">1 tuple</span>

<!-- 3. OOB swap: clears any old validation message in id="form-errors" -->
<div id="form-errors" hx-swap-oob="innerHTML"></div>
HTMX 4 processes the primary #tuple-list swap first, then pulls out any top-level nodes with hx-swap-oob and swaps each one into the matching #id elsewhere on the page.

Why Use It
One round-trip: You update the main list, a counter badge, and a status/error banner in a single HTTP response.

No layout coupling: #tuple-count and #form-errors can live anywhere in your layout (header, sidebar, footer) without forcing you to re-render the whole page.

Explain the difference between hx-swap-oob=

It looks like your message cut off right after hx-swap-oob=—assuming you want to compare the different hx-swap-oob modes in HTMX 4 (true, innerHTML, explicit selectors, and morph swaps), here is how they differ.

Core hx-swap-oob Modes
Attribute Value	What Gets Swapped	Best For
hx-swap-oob="true"	Replaces the entire matching element (outerHTML by default), including its own attributes and tag.	Standalone badges or status widgets where you want to replace the whole element node.
hx-swap-oob="innerHTML"	Keeps the existing element in the DOM and replaces only its inner content.	Counters (#tuple-count), banners (#form-errors), or containers where keeping the wrapper element and its id is cleaner.
hx-swap-oob="innerHTML:#target" or hx-swap-oob="#target"	Targets an explicit CSS selector instead of matching strictly by the OOB element's own id.	Updating a target whose ID differs from the fragment element, or targeting a specific sub-element cleanly.
hx-swap-oob="innerMorph" / outerMorph	Patches/morphs existing DOM nodes in place rather than tearing down and recreating them.	Preserving focus, input state, or active CSS transitions on elements that update frequently.
hx-swap-oob="beforeend" / afterbegin / delete	Appends, prepends, or removes a target element without replacing its whole body.	Appending log entries, inserting notifications at the top of a list, or removing a dismissed alert.
Concrete Example: true vs. innerHTML
Suppose your page already has:

HTML
<span id="tuple-count" class="badge">3 tuples</span>
With hx-swap-oob="innerHTML": The server sends <span id="tuple-count" hx-swap-oob="innerHTML">4 tuples</span>. HTMX keeps the existing <span id="tuple-count" class="badge"> in place and swaps only 4 tuples inside it.

With hx-swap-oob="true": The server sends <span id="tuple-count" hx-swap-oob="true">4 tuples</span>. HTMX replaces the entire existing <span> node with the new <span> node—which loses any client-side attributes or classes that existed only in the live DOM unless the server reproduces them every time.

Rule of Thumb for go-topaz-ui
Prefer hx-swap-oob="innerHTML" on stable containers (#tuple-count, #form-errors, #graph-banner) so the container itself stays put.

Use innerMorph / outerMorph when an OOB-updated region might hold focus or ongoing UI state.

Reserve hx-swap-oob="true" for self-contained elements where full node replacement is intentional.

